{"name":"Kirby","hex":"#d74894","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Kirby","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["An English placename.","A habitational surname from Old English originally for a person from Kirby."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Kirby"}],"entries":[{"title":"Kirby (series)","blocks":[{"text":"Kirby is a media franchise created by Japanese video game designer and director Masahiro Sakurai. The franchise revolves around the titular protagonist and his adventures on the fictional planet Pop Star and beyond. The franchise is primarily developed by HAL Laboratory and published by Nintendo, with both companies jointly owning the property."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ee/Kirby_blue_and_yellow_logo.png/330px-Kirby_blue_and_yellow_logo.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kirby_(series)"},{"title":"Kirby (character)","blocks":[{"text":"Kirby  is the titular character and protagonist of the Kirby series by HAL Laboratory. He first appeared in Kirby's Dream Land (1992), a platform game for the Game Boy. Since then, Kirby has appeared in over 50 games, ranging from action platformers to puzzle, racing, and pinball, and has been featured as a playable character in every installment of the Super Smash Bros. series (1999–present). He has also starred in his own anime and manga series. Since 1999, he has been voiced by Makiko Ohmoto."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/4/4e/Kirby_Nintendo.png/330px-Kirby_Nintendo.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kirby_(character)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/60/Kirby_gets_an_ability_%28videogame_gameplay%29.png/330px-Kirby_gets_an_ability_%28videogame_gameplay%29.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/60/Kirby_gets_an_ability_%28videogame_gameplay%29.png/330px-Kirby_gets_an_ability_%28videogame_gameplay%29.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Kirby can gain the properties of enemies or objects he eats – for example, by inhaling a sword-wielding enemy, Kirby gains the Sword ability.[10]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AKirby_gets_an_ability_(videogame_gameplay).png"},{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/4/41/Kirbys_Dream_Land_Whispy.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/4/41/Kirbys_Dream_Land_Whispy.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"Kirby's Dream Land, released in 1992 for the Game Boy, was the first game in the series.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AKirbys_Dream_Land_Whispy.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/4/4c/Kirby64PowerCombo.jpg/250px-Kirby64PowerCombo.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/4/4c/Kirby64PowerCombo.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/4/4c/Kirby64PowerCombo.jpg/250px-Kirby64PowerCombo.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Kirby 64: The Crystal Shards, released in 2000 for the Nintendo 64, introduced 3D graphics to the series, while retaining the classic 2D side-scrolling gameplay.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AKirby64PowerCombo.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/5/51/Kirbycanvascurse4.jpg/250px-Kirbycanvascurse4.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/5/51/Kirbycanvascurse4.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/5/51/Kirbycanvascurse4.jpg/250px-Kirbycanvascurse4.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Kirby: Canvas Curse, released in 2005 for the Nintendo DS, introduced touch-based gameplay to the series.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AKirbycanvascurse4.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/a/a2/Kirby_rtdl_wii.jpg/330px-Kirby_rtdl_wii.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/a/a2/Kirby_rtdl_wii.jpg/330px-Kirby_rtdl_wii.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Kirby's Return to Dream Land, released in 2011 for the Wii, returned traditional platforming gameplay to the series – the first in a home console since Kirby 64: The Crystal Shards (2000) – featuring local multiplayer up to 4 players.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AKirby_rtdl_wii.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/c/c6/Kirby_Characters_in_Smash.jpg/330px-Kirby_Characters_in_Smash.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/c/c6/Kirby_Characters_in_Smash.jpg/330px-Kirby_Characters_in_Smash.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Left to right: Meta Knight, King Dedede and Kirby in Super Smash Bros. Brawl","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AKirby_Characters_in_Smash.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/4/4e/Kirby_Nintendo.png/330px-Kirby_Nintendo.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/4/4e/Kirby_Nintendo.png/330px-Kirby_Nintendo.png?utm_source=en.wikip